# _headers — the site's response headers (SOURCE file; see the serving note
# below).
#
# Like _redirects, this is the single SOURCE file and the @astrojs/cloudflare
# adapter merges into it at build: it PREPENDS its own `/_astro/*` immutable
# Cache-Control block above everything here (watch for the "Injected immutable
# Cache-Control for /_astro/*" build log line). The built dist/client/_headers
# is this file plus that block — never hand-write the asset caching rule.
#
# ⚠ COMMENTS GO AT COLUMN 0, NEVER INDENTED INSIDE A RULE BLOCK. Indentation is
#   the only thing that distinguishes a header line from a path line, and
#   Cloudflare's documented syntax shows `#` comments only at column 0 — an
#   indented comment is therefore in header position, and a parser that doesn't
#   special-case it reads it as a malformed header rather than skipping it. That
#   would fail silently, on the one file whose entire job is security headers.
#   So the annotations for each header live below, not beside it, and the block
#   itself stays pure `name: value`.
#
# ⚠ FOOTGUN — never put a `Cache-Control` line in a broad block like `/*`.
#   Before injecting, the adapter probes this file for a Cache-Control on any
#   rule matching `/_astro/probe`; `/*` compiles to `^.*$`, so it matches. On a
#   hit the adapter SKIPS its injection entirely and says so only at DEBUG level
#   — a normal build looks completely clean while a year of immutable asset
#   caching silently disappears. If you ever need per-path caching, scope the
#   pattern so it cannot match `/_astro/*`.
#   (Adapter source: @astrojs/cloudflare/dist/utils/headers.js.)
#
# ⚠ NOT SERVED DIRECTLY. Amplify does not read this file — the serving copy is
#   customHttp.yml, ported BY HAND, so any header change here must be MIRRORED
#   there. This file stays the source of truth: audit:headers validates it, and
#   the smoke check's security-headers row is what catches the two drifting on
#   a live host. The one on-demand route (src/pages/api/create-donation.ts)
#   inherits nothing from either file — it sets the same headers itself, in its
#   `json` helper. Anything added below that should also cover the API has to
#   be added there too.
#
# Format limits (enforced by audit:headers): 100 rules, 2,000 characters per line.
#
# Pre-cutover baseline: the WordPress site sent NONE of these headers, so
# everything below is net-new rather than parity.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE:
#
#   A real Content-Security-Policy (`script-src` / `style-src`). Shiki emits
#   per-token inline `style` ATTRIBUTES, which CSP hashes cannot cover, so it
#   needs its own Report-Only rollout — see BACKLOG.md §13. The one CSP
#   directive below is the exception that proves the rule: `frame-ancestors`
#   governs who may embed US and has no bearing on what we may load, so it
#   carries none of that breakage risk. Do NOT append script-src/style-src to
#   that line; that's the §13 project, not a one-line edit.
#
#   Strict-Transport-Security. An edge-level decision, deliberately deferred
#   (see HOSTING.md → Response headers): hold off on `includeSubDomains` until
#   every *.openmined.org subdomain is confirmed HTTPS-ready (blog., courses.,
#   design., syft.docs. all exist, and blog content still carries http:// links
#   to slack.openmined.org), and treat `preload` as a separate, effectively
#   irreversible decision.
#
#   Cache-Control. See the footgun above.
#
# THE RULE BELOW, HEADER BY HEADER:
#
#   X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
#     Stop the browser second-guessing a response's declared Content-Type.
#
#   Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
#     Full URL to ourselves; cross-origin gets only the origin; HTTPS→HTTP gets
#     nothing. Matters here because outbound links from posts would otherwise
#     hand the full reading URL to third parties.
#
#   Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
#   X-Frame-Options: DENY
#     Clickjacking — nobody may frame our pages, which matters because the
#     donate trigger is a one-click control on most of them. frame-ancestors is
#     the modern form; X-Frame-Options is the fallback for older browsers. We
#     frame OTHERS (HubSpot forms, YouTube in 4 posts) and that is unaffected —
#     this only restricts being framed. Stripe Checkout is a top-level
#     redirect, not a frame.
#
#   Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
#     Powerful APIs the site never uses — off by default rather than on request.
#
#   Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
#     Severs the window.opener link for anything we open. `-allow-popups` is the
#     conservative variant, chosen over bare `same-origin` because nothing here
#     needs the stricter isolation (no SharedArrayBuffer) and the stricter value
#     is the one that breaks popups.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
